<Project>

  <!--
    Razor source generator workaround.

    The Razor source generator shipped in .NET SDK 10.0.300 and later falsely reports
    RZ1021 ("Markup in a code block must start with a tag") for perfectly valid markup:
    any HTML element inside a Razor code block (@if / @foreach / ...) can be mis-parsed,
    and the rest of the line is emitted as raw C#. One bad .cshtml then cascades into
    100+ CS errors across the whole assembly, so the real cause is easy to miss.

    Upstream issue: https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/85656
    Reproduced here on SDK 10.0.401 with <strong> inside @if, <br /><small> on one line,
    <text> in a block, and <script src="..."> in a block.

    Switching the Razor compile step back to the non-source-generator path avoids the
    faulty parser. This is the workaround named in the upstream issue; it changes only
    how .cshtml files are turned into C#, not what the views render.

    Remove this file once the SDK is updated to a version where the bug is fixed.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseRazorSourceGenerator>false</UseRazorSourceGenerator>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
